<!-- Provenance: captured with the SEMPv1 show-redundancy request during a
     release-activity window on a real Active/Standby HA pair (SOL-151996).
     This is the DEGRADED-state fixture, and the counterpart to
     show_redundancy_active_standby.xml: where that one is a healthy pair seen
     from its active Primary member, this one is a pair whose Primary has
     released activity, seen from the Backup member that took over.

     What only this fixture exercises:
       - redundancy-status Down while both adb-link-up and adb-hello-up are
         still true. Mate-link health and group health are independent — a
         reader that infers one from the other is wrong, and this is the only
         fixture that separates them (the standalone fixture has Down with both
         flags false, so it cannot).
       - active-standby-role Backup. Every other fixture is Primary.
       - a fully populated <backup> virtual-router status subtree, with the
         <primary> subtree reduced to bare <activity>. The exact mirror of
         show_redundancy_active_standby.xml, so the omitempty behavior is
         pinned in both directions rather than only ever on the backup role.
       - priority-reported-by-mate reporting "Release" / "Release (0)". No
         other fixture carries this enum value.

     How it was obtained, since it is not reproducible from a Cloud service by
     observation alone: `release-activity` under configure/redundancy on the
     active Primary member, then a capture through the service's management
     endpoint. A Cloud HA service fronts one endpoint that routes to whichever
     member is active, so releasing activity moves that endpoint onto the
     Backup member — which is what makes the populated backup subtree
     reachable without direct access to the standby. Reverted afterwards with
     `no release-activity`; recovery is not instant (the returning member
     resyncs its message spool, ~45s here) and redundancy-status reads Down
     until it completes.

     Note: config-status is "Enabled", not the "Enabled-Released" the released
     member's CLI reports. Not a parse gap — the release is configured on the
     Primary, while this capture is from the Backup, which carries no such
     flag. A capture from the released member itself would differ here.

     Scrubbed capture: group-management-server-identity, mate-router-name, and
     the VRRP static-address are synthetic placeholders, not the real service's
     identity. Do not paste a raw re-capture over them. The mate name keeps the
     broker's real "<identity-prefix>primary" pairing — note the suffix is
     "primary" here, not "backup", because the mate of the Backup member is the
     Primary. The static-address is .11 where the active-standby fixture is
     .10: these are two different members of the pair. Placeholder
     conventions: test/performance/mock-semp/canned/sanitize.local.tsv.example -->
<rpc-reply semp-version="broker/10.25.24.8245">
  <rpc>
    <show>
      <redundancy>
        <config-status>Enabled</config-status>
        <redundancy-status>Down</redundancy-status>
        <operating-mode>Message Routing Node</operating-mode>
        <switchover-mechanism>Hostlist</switchover-mechanism>
        <auto-revert>false</auto-revert>
        <redundancy-mode>Active/Standby</redundancy-mode>
        <active-standby-role>Backup</active-standby-role>
        <group-management-server-identity>testha01.messaging.example.com</group-management-server-identity>
        <mate-router-name>testha01primary</mate-router-name>
        <oper-status>
          <adb-link-up>true</adb-link-up>
          <adb-hello-up>true</adb-hello-up>
        </oper-status>
        <failover-criteria>any-fail</failover-criteria>
        <vrrp-interfaces>
          <interface>
            <name>intf0</name>
            <static-address>198.51.100.11</static-address>
            <static-status>Up</static-status>
          </interface>
        </vrrp-interfaces>
        <virtual-routers>
          <primary>
            <config>
              <routing-interface>intf0:1</routing-interface>
              <vrrp-vrid>-1</vrrp-vrid>
            </config>
            <status>
              <activity>Shutdown</activity>
            </status>
          </primary>
          <backup>
            <config>
              <routing-interface>intf0:1</routing-interface>
              <vrrp-vrid>-1</vrrp-vrid>
            </config>
            <status>
              <activity>Local Active</activity>
              <vrrp>Initialize</vrrp>
              <vrrp-interfaces>
                <interface>
                  <name>intf0</name>
                  <status>Initialize</status>
                </interface>
              </vrrp-interfaces>
              <routing-interface>Up</routing-interface>
              <vrrp-priority>240</vrrp-priority>
              <priority-reported-by-mate>
                <summary>Release</summary>
                <cspf>None</cspf>
                <adb-hello>Release</adb-hello>
                <vrrp>Release (0)</vrrp>
                <vrrp-interfaces>
                  <interface>
                    <name>intf0</name>
                    <priority>Release (0)</priority>
                  </interface>
                </vrrp-interfaces>
              </priority-reported-by-mate>
            </status>
          </backup>
        </virtual-routers>
      </redundancy>
    </show>
  </rpc>
<execute-result code="ok"/>
</rpc-reply>
